    Table 4 also shows the proportions of persons aged 15-64 years and 65 years and over, by district.
    The 15-64 year age group is commonly considered as the working age group especially in more
    developed countries. In Baghlan Province, the working age group accounted for 51.1 percent of the
    total population. At the district level, this proportion ranged from 47.9 percent in Firing Wa Gharu to
    60.3 percent in Dahana-E-Ghuri.
    When older persons (65 years old and over) comprise less than 10 percent of the total population, it is
    also considered a young population. The proportion of older or aged persons for Baghlan Province in
    2016 was 2.1 percent, with district level proportions varying from 1.3 percent in Baghlan-E-Jadeed to
    3.3 percent in Firing Wa Gharu.
    The aged-child ratio or the ratio of persons aged 65 years and over to the number of children less
    than 15 years is also shown in Table 4. In Baghaln Province, the aged-child ratio was 4.4, which
    means that in 2016, there were more than four persons aged 65 years and over for every 100 children
    under 15 years of age. At the district level, the ratio ranged from 2.5 percent in Baghlan-E-Jadeed to
    7.3 percent in Dushi.
    Another indicator of population composition is the age dependency ratio. This ratio shows changes
    commonly calculated as the sum of the number of children and the number of aged or older persons
    divided by the number of working-age population. It is observed that young populations typically have
    a total dependency ratio exceeding 100.
